[0.000, "o", "+const { parse } = z.string();\r\n   +\r\n   +parse(\"some data\");\r\n   +```\r\n   +\r\n   +Binding a method allocates a closure per instance, so Zod defers it. Methods are defined as *getters* on the prototype. The first time a method is accessed on an instance, the getter returns an implementation bound to that instance and assigns it as an [own property](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/hasOwnProperty), so later accesses resolve directly. A method that is never used is never allocated.\r\n   +\r\n   +```ts\r\n   +const s = z.string();\r\n   +Object.getOwnPropertyNames(s);\r\n   +// => [\"_zod\", \"def\", \"type\", \"format\", \"minLength\", \"maxLength\"]\r\n   +\r\n   +s.parse; // materializes the bound method\r\n   +Object.getOwnPropertyNames(s).length; // 7\r\n   +```\r\n   +\r\n   +This is why a schema stays small. V8 sizes an object's property store in steps — 128 bytes up to 12 own properties, 848 bytes from 13, 1616 bytes from 21 — and a schema in Zod 4.4 carried 49 own properties, 40"]
